    "thm-adhm-fixed-point-instanton-count": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-qftx-adhm-linear-data-stability-and-regularity",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Fix integers $N\\ge1$ and $k\\ge0$ and standard Hermitian spaces $V=\\mathbb C^k$, $W=\\mathbb C^N$. A framed ADHM linear datum is $x=(B_1,B_2,I,J)$ with $B_i\\in\\operatorname{End}_{\\mathbb C}(V)$, $I:W\\to V$ and $J:V\\to W$. Define the complex and real polynomial expressions\n\n$$\\mu_{\\mathbb C}(x)=[B_1,B_2]+IJ,\\qquad \\mu_{\\mathbb R}(x)=[B_1,B_1^\\dagger]+[B_2,B_2^\\dagger]+II^\\dagger-J^\\dagger J.$$\n\nThe datum is stable if the vectors $B_{i_1}\\cdots B_{i_m}I(w)$, with $m\\ge0$, $i_j\\in\\{1,2\\}$ and $w\\in W$, span $V$. It is costable if there is no nonzero subspace of $V$ invariant under both $B_i$ and contained in $\\ker J$. It is regular if it is stable and costable. The group $GL(V)$ acts by $g\\cdot x=(gB_1g^{-1},gB_2g^{-1},gI,Jg^{-1})$. Its unitary subgroup is $U(V)$.\n\nWrite $X^s=\\{x:\\mu_{\\mathbb C}(x)=0,\\ x\\text{ stable}\\}$, $X^{\\mathrm{reg}}=\\{x\\in X^s:x\\text{ costable}\\}$ and, for real $\\zeta\\ge0$, $X_\\zeta^U=\\{x\\in X^s:\\mu_{\\mathbb R}(x)=\\zeta I_V\\}$. Denote the corresponding orbit sets by $X^s/GL(V)$, $X^{\\mathrm{reg}}/GL(V)$ and $X_\\zeta^U/U(V)$, with quotient topologies when discussing their analytic spaces. A word frame consists of $k$ columns obtained by fixed words in $B_1,B_2$ applied to fixed standard basis vectors of $W$.\n\nFor $T_{\\mathbb C}=(\\mathbb C^*)^2\\times(\\mathbb C^*)^N$, write $t_1,t_2,e_1,\\ldots,e_N$ for the coordinate characters and put $h=\\operatorname{diag}(e_1^{-1},\\ldots,e_N^{-1})$. Its action is $(t_1,t_2,h)\\cdot x=(t_1B_1,t_2B_2,Ih^{-1},t_1t_2hJ)$. The compact torus is its subgroup on which all characters have modulus one. The framing representation thus has character $\\sum_\\alpha e_\\alpha^{-1}$ and its dual has character $\\sum_\\alpha e_\\alpha$.\n\nThese are finite-dimensional matrix definitions. They do not assert a real/complex quotient correspondence, a sheaf or ASD identification, or a noncompact pushforward. In particular, positive-FI stability and zero-FI regularity are distinct conditions; neither an endpoint identification nor an ordinary ASD interpretation follows from this definition.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-atiyah-bott-berline-vergne-integration-formula",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Work in $\\mathrm{ZF}+\\mathrm{AC}$. Let $T$ be a compact torus acting smoothly on a compact oriented smooth $n$-manifold $M$ without boundary; use the standard Hausdorff, second-countable manifold convention, and assume the action preserves the orientation. Write $F=M^T=\\coprod_{j=1}^{s}F_j$ for its fixed locus, with $s<\\infty$, and $i_j:F_j\\hookrightarrow M$ for each connected component. Give each normal bundle $\\nu_j$ a $T$-equivariant orientation and orient $F_j$ by $o(TM|_{F_j})=o(TF_j)\\wedge o(\\nu_j)$. Use real Borel equivariant cohomology, $\\mathcal R=H^*(BT;\\mathbb R)$, the multiplicative set $S$ generated by nonzero character classes, and the degree-shifting fiber-integration convention of the preceding definition. For every homogeneous $\\alpha\\in H_T^k(M;\\mathbb R)$,\n\n$$\\int_M\\alpha=\\sum_j\\int_{F_j}\\frac{i_j^*\\alpha}{e_T(\\nu_j)}\\quad\\text{in }S^{-1}H^{k-n}(BT;\\mathbb R).$$\n\nIf the fixed locus is nonempty and consists of isolated points, necessarily $n=2m$. Use the ambient orientation on each normal space $\\nu_p=T_pM$ and the positive orientation on each point $p$. Choose a $T$-invariant complex structure on each $T_pM$ and let $\\chi_{p,1},\\ldots,\\chi_{p,m}$ be its nonzero character weights. Let $\\epsilon_p=+1$ when its complex orientation agrees with the orientation of $M$ at $p$ and $-1$ otherwise. Then the same formula specializes to\n\n$$\\int_M\\alpha=\\sum_{p\\in M^T}\\frac{\\epsilon_p\\,i_p^*\\alpha}{\\prod_{a=1}^{m}u_{\\chi_{p,a}}}\\quad\\text{in }S^{-1}\\mathcal R.$$\n\nIf the fixed locus is empty, the fixed-component formula is the empty sum $0$ for every $n$; no parity condition or integer $m$ is needed in that case.\n\nThis is a finite-dimensional formula for compact manifolds; it does not assert a formula for arbitrary noncompact instanton spaces or infinite-dimensional field spaces.",
          "uses": [
            "2.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "lem-qftx-stable-adhm-partition-fixed-points-and-tangent-character",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume full AC. Fix integers $N\\geq1$ and $k\\geq0$ and a positive real stability parameter $\\zeta$. Let $V=\\mathbb C^k$ and $W=\\mathbb C^N$ have their standard Hermitian structures. Define $\\mathcal M^\\zeta_{k,N}$ to be the geometric quotient by $GL(V)$ of data $(B_1,B_2,I,J)$ with $B_i\\in\\operatorname{End}(V)$, $I\\in\\operatorname{Hom}(W,V)$, $J\\in\\operatorname{Hom}(V,W)$, $[B_1,B_2]+IJ=0$, and $\\operatorname{span}\\{B_{i_1}\\cdots B_{i_m}I(w):m\\geq0,\\ w\\in W\\}=V$. The positive parameter $\\zeta$ specifies the positive-FI stability chamber. Let $T=(S^1)^2\\times(S^1)^N$, with rotation characters $t_1,t_2$ and dual framing characters $e_1,\\ldots,e_N$. Thus $W$ has character $\\mathbf W=\\sum_\\alpha e_\\alpha^{-1}$ and $W^\\vee$ has character $\\mathbf W^\\vee=\\sum_\\alpha e_\\alpha$. Its action is $(t_1,t_2,h)\\cdot(B_1,B_2,I,J)=(t_1B_1,t_2B_2,Ih^{-1},t_1t_2hJ)$, where $h$ acts on $W$ with weights $e_\\alpha^{-1}$. Work over the rational function field $K=\\mathbb Q(\\epsilon_1,\\epsilon_2,a_1,\\ldots,a_N)$ in algebraically independent variables. For $\\chi=t_1^{m_1}t_2^{m_2}\\prod_\\alpha e_\\alpha^{n_\\alpha}$ set $u_\\chi=m_1\\epsilon_1+m_2\\epsilon_2+\\sum_\\alpha n_\\alpha a_\\alpha$.\n\nFor a partition $Y$, a Young diagram means a finite downward-closed subset of $\\mathbb Z_{\\geq0}^2$; write $|Y|$ for its number of boxes. For an $N$-tuple $\\vec Y=(Y_1,\\ldots,Y_N)$ set $|\\vec Y|=\\sum_\\alpha|Y_\\alpha|$, $\\mathbf W=\\sum_\\alpha e_\\alpha^{-1}$, $\\mathbf W^\\vee=\\sum_\\alpha e_\\alpha$, and\n$$\\mathbf V_{\\vec Y}=\\sum_{\\alpha=1}^N e_\\alpha\\sum_{(i,j)\\in Y_\\alpha}t_1^it_2^j.$$\nThen $\\mathcal M^\\zeta_{k,N}$ is smooth of complex dimension $2kN$, and its $T$-fixed points are isolated and in bijection with the $N$-tuples of partitions of total size $k$. At the fixed point $\\vec Y$, the tangent character is\n$$\\operatorname{ch}_T(T_{\\vec Y}\\mathcal M^\\zeta_{k,N})=\\mathbf V_{\\vec Y}^{\\vee}\\mathbf V_{\\vec Y}(t_1+t_2-1-t_1t_2)+\\mathbf V_{\\vec Y}^{\\vee}\\mathbf W^\\vee+t_1t_2\\mathbf W\\mathbf V_{\\vec Y}.$$\nLet $E_{\\vec Y}$ be the product of $u_\\chi$ over the tangent weights counted with multiplicity; the fixed points are isolated, so none of these weights is zero. Define the fixed-point series by\n$$Z^{\\mathrm{fp}}_N(q)=\\sum_{k\\geq0}q^k\\sum_{|\\vec Y|=k}E_{\\vec Y}^{-1}\\in K\\langle\\!\\langle q\\rangle\\!\\rangle.$$\nEach coefficient is a finite sum, and the series is formal; no analytic convergence is asserted.\n\nThe quotient is a separated smooth finite-type complex algebraic variety with a finite invariant affine word-slice atlas for the algebraic complex torus $T_{\\mathbb C}=(\\mathbb C^*)^{N+2}$. This result concerns the stable matrix quotient and a formal series. It asserts no sheaf/ASD dictionary or integration identity.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "L1",
          "source": "thm-atiyah-bott-berline-vergne-integration-formula",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Work in $\\mathrm{ZF}+\\mathrm{AC}$. Let $T$ be a compact torus acting smoothly on a compact oriented smooth $n$-manifold $M$ without boundary; use the standard Hausdorff, second-countable manifold convention, and assume the action preserves the orientation. Write $F=M^T=\\coprod_{j=1}^{s}F_j$ for its fixed locus, with $s<\\infty$, and $i_j:F_j\\hookrightarrow M$ for each connected component. Give each normal bundle $\\nu_j$ a $T$-equivariant orientation and orient $F_j$ by $o(TM|_{F_j})=o(TF_j)\\wedge o(\\nu_j)$. Use real Borel equivariant cohomology, $\\mathcal R=H^*(BT;\\mathbb R)$, the multiplicative set $S$ generated by nonzero character classes, and the degree-shifting fiber-integration convention of the preceding definition. For every homogeneous $\\alpha\\in H_T^k(M;\\mathbb R)$,\n\n$$\\int_M\\alpha=\\sum_j\\int_{F_j}\\frac{i_j^*\\alpha}{e_T(\\nu_j)}\\quad\\text{in }S^{-1}H^{k-n}(BT;\\mathbb R).$$\n\nIf the fixed locus is nonempty and consists of isolated points, necessarily $n=2m$. Use the ambient orientation on each normal space $\\nu_p=T_pM$ and the positive orientation on each point $p$. Choose a $T$-invariant complex structure on each $T_pM$ and let $\\chi_{p,1},\\ldots,\\chi_{p,m}$ be its nonzero character weights. Let $\\epsilon_p=+1$ when its complex orientation agrees with the orientation of $M$ at $p$ and $-1$ otherwise. Then the same formula specializes to\n\n$$\\int_M\\alpha=\\sum_{p\\in M^T}\\frac{\\epsilon_p\\,i_p^*\\alpha}{\\prod_{a=1}^{m}u_{\\chi_{p,a}}}\\quad\\text{in }S^{-1}\\mathcal R.$$\n\nIf the fixed locus is empty, the fixed-component formula is the empty sum $0$ for every $n$; no parity condition or integer $m$ is needed in that case.\n\nThis is a finite-dimensional formula for compact manifolds; it does not assert a formula for arbitrary noncompact instanton spaces or infinite-dimensional field spaces.",
          "uses": [
            "2.3",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "L2",
          "source": "def-framed-u-n-instanton-moduli-and-adhm-data",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Choice. Let $N\\geq 2$ and let $S^4=\\mathbb R^4\\cup\\{\\infty\\}$ be the one-point compactification, oriented so the coordinates $x^1,x^2,x^3,x^4$ on $\\mathbb R^4$ have $dx^1\\wedge dx^2\\wedge dx^3\\wedge dx^4>0$. Give $S^4$ a smooth round metric in the conformal class of the Euclidean metric on $\\mathbb R^4$. A framed $U(N)$ connection is a smooth unitary connection $A$ on a Hermitian rank-$N$ vector bundle $E\\to S^4$, together with a chosen unitary frame $\\Phi:E_\\infty\\to\\mathbb C^N$ of its fiber at $\\infty$. Gauge transformations are smooth unitary bundle automorphisms that equal the identity in this frame at $\\infty$. The curvature is $F_A$, and the Hodge operator is defined by $\\alpha\\wedge *_g\\beta=\\langle\\alpha,\\beta\\rangle_g\\,d\\mathrm{vol}_g$ on two-forms. Set $F_A^+=(F_A+*_gF_A)/2$ and $F_A^-=(F_A-*_gF_A)/2$; the anti-self-dual condition is $F_A^+=0$, and the self-dual condition is $F_A^-=0$.\n\nLet $\\mathfrak u(N)$ be the anti-Hermitian matrices and put $B_N(X,Y)=-\\operatorname{tr}_N(XY)$. In the trace convention of the library, the charge of $A$ is $q_N(A)=(8\\pi^2)^{-1}\\int_{S^4}B_N(F_A\\wedge F_A)$, and the positive anti-self-dual instanton number is $k(A)=-q_N(A)$. For $k\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\geq0}$, the framed anti-self-dual charge-$k$ moduli set $\\mathcal I^{\\mathrm{ASD}}_{k,N}$ consists of framed pairs $(E,A)$ with $F_A^+=0$ and $k(A)=k$, modulo the stated based gauge transformations. The integer-charge condition is part of the selected bundle sector; no analytic existence or uniqueness assertion is included in this definition. The trace and Hodge conventions agree with the supplied compact-four-manifold definition of $q_B$; its special $SU(2)$ integrality clause is not used to assert $U(N)$ integrality.\n\nFor $k\\geq0$, take Hermitian spaces $V=\\mathbb C^k$ and $W=\\mathbb C^N$. The ADHM data are\n$$B_1,B_2\\in\\operatorname{End}_{\\mathbb C}(V),\\qquad I\\in\\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathbb C}(W,V),\\qquad J\\in\\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathbb C}(V,W).$$\nTheir data space is $\\mathcal R_{k,N}=\\operatorname{End}_{\\mathbb C}(V)^2\\oplus\\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathbb C}(W,V)\\oplus\\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathbb C}(V,W)$, and their complex and real moment-map expressions are\n$$\\mu_{\\mathbb C}=[B_1,B_2]+IJ\\in\\operatorname{End}_{\\mathbb C}(V),\\qquad \\mu_{\\mathbb R}=[B_1,B_1^\\dagger]+[B_2,B_2^\\dagger]+II^\\dagger-J^\\dagger J\\in\\operatorname{End}_{\\mathbb C}(V).$$\nHere $[X,Y]=XY-YX$, and $\\dagger$ is the Hermitian adjoint. The two displayed formulas define maps $\\mu_{\\mathbb C}:\\mathcal R_{k,N}\\to\\operatorname{End}_{\\mathbb C}(V)$ and $\\mu_{\\mathbb R}:\\mathcal R_{k,N}\\to\\operatorname{Herm}(V)$, where $\\mathcal R_{k,N}$ is the stated product of four Hom spaces and $\\operatorname{Herm}(V)$ is the real vector space of Hermitian endomorphisms. They are the complex and real ADHM moment-map expressions, with these formulas fixing their normalization. A datum is stable when\n$$\\operatorname{span}_{\\mathbb C}\\{B_{i_1}\\cdots B_{i_m}I(w):m\\geq0,\\ i_j\\in\\{1,2\\},\\ w\\in W\\}=V,$$\nwhere the word of length zero is the identity. Equivalently, no proper $B_1,B_2$-invariant subspace of $V$ contains $\\operatorname{im}I$. The group $GL(V)$ consists of invertible complex-linear endomorphisms, and $U(V)$ is its subgroup preserving the chosen Hermitian inner product. The group $GL(V)$ acts by $g\\cdot(B_1,B_2,I,J)=(gB_1g^{-1},gB_2g^{-1},gI,Jg^{-1})$. For $\\zeta>0$, the stable complex ADHM quotient $\\mathcal M^{\\mathrm{GIT},\\zeta}_{k,N}$ is the geometric quotient of $\\{\\mu_{\\mathbb C}=0,\\ \\text{stable}\\}$ by $GL(V)$ in the positive stability chamber; $\\zeta$ labels that chamber and is not an additional coordinate on the complex equation set.\n\nFor a real parameter $\\zeta\\geq0$, the real moment-map presentation is the quotient of $\\{\\mu_{\\mathbb C}=0,\\ \\mu_{\\mathbb R}=\\zeta\\,\\mathrm{id}_V,\\ \\text{stable}\\}$ by $U(V)$. At $\\zeta>0$ this is called the resolved or positive-FI ADHM quotient; at $\\zeta=0$ its affine completion includes the small-instanton locus. The equations and quotient are definitions; this item does not assert that the real moment-map quotient is isomorphic to the stable complex quotient or to an instanton moduli space.\n\nWrite $\\mathbb{CP}^2$ for the complex projective plane, $\\ell_\\infty=\\{[0:z_1:z_2]\\}$ for its line at infinity, and $\\mathcal O_{\\ell_\\infty}$ for the sheaf of holomorphic functions on that line. The affine chart $\\mathbb{CP}^2\\setminus\\ell_\\infty$ is $\\mathbb C^2$; projective compactification adds the whole line $\\ell_\\infty$, unlike the one-point compactification $S^4$. Let $\\mathcal O(1)$ be the hyperplane line bundle and $\\mathcal O(m)=\\mathcal O(1)^{\\otimes m}$ for $m\\geq0$. A coherent sheaf is a sheaf of $\\mathcal O_{\\mathbb{CP}^2}$-modules that is locally finitely presented; it is torsion-free when no nonzero local section is annihilated by a nonzero local function. Its rank is the dimension of its generic fiber over the function field. Define its Hilbert polynomial by $P_E(m)=\\sum_{i=0}^2(-1)^i\\dim_{\\mathbb C}H^i(\\mathbb{CP}^2,E\\otimes\\mathcal O(m))$ for all sufficiently large integers $m$. The framed Gieseker moduli space $\\mathcal G_{k,N}$ is the moduli of pairs $(E,\\Phi)$ with $E$ a rank-$N$ torsion-free coherent sheaf, $P_E(m)=N\\binom{m+2}{2}-k$, and $\\Phi:E|_{\\ell_\\infty}\\cong\\mathcal O_{\\ell_\\infty}^{\\oplus N}$. Its locally-free locus is distinguished from the boundary of non-locally-free sheaves. The Uhlenbeck set-theoretic compactification is the disjoint union of strata $\\mathcal I^{\\mathrm{ASD}}_{k-\\ell,N}\\times\\operatorname{Sym}^{\\ell}(\\mathbb R^4)$ for $0\\leq\\ell\\leq k$, where $\\operatorname{Sym}^{\\ell}(\\mathbb R^4)=(\\mathbb R^4)^\\ell/S_\\ell$ records ideal point defects. The Gieseker space and the Uhlenbeck space are distinct: the former retains torsion-free sheaf data at the small-instanton boundary, while the latter records only ideal points. No compactness of either full space is asserted here.\n\nWrite $z_1=x^1+ix^2$ and $z_2=x^3+ix^4$. The compact torus $T=(S^1)^2\\times T_G$, with $T_G\\subset U(W)$ the diagonal framing torus, acts on ADHM data by\n$$(t_1,t_2,h)\\cdot(B_1,B_2,I,J)=(t_1B_1,t_2B_2,Ih^{-1},t_1t_2hJ).$$\nThis convention makes $\\mu_{\\mathbb C}$ transform with character $t_1t_2$ and leaves $\\mu_{\\mathbb R}$ invariant. Let $e_\\alpha$ denote the character of the dual of the $\\alpha$th framing line, so the framing representation has character $\\mathbf W=\\sum_{\\alpha=1}^N e_\\alpha^{-1}$ and its dual has character $\\mathbf W^\\vee=\\sum_{\\alpha=1}^N e_\\alpha$. For the rotation characters write $t_1,t_2$, and write $\\epsilon_i=c_1^T(t_i)$ and $a_\\alpha=c_1^T(e_\\alpha)$ for their additive equivariant weights.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "2.2",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "L2",
          "source": "def-qftx-adhm-linear-data-stability-and-regularity",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Fix integers $N\\ge1$ and $k\\ge0$ and standard Hermitian spaces $V=\\mathbb C^k$, $W=\\mathbb C^N$. A framed ADHM linear datum is $x=(B_1,B_2,I,J)$ with $B_i\\in\\operatorname{End}_{\\mathbb C}(V)$, $I:W\\to V$ and $J:V\\to W$. Define the complex and real polynomial expressions\n\n$$\\mu_{\\mathbb C}(x)=[B_1,B_2]+IJ,\\qquad \\mu_{\\mathbb R}(x)=[B_1,B_1^\\dagger]+[B_2,B_2^\\dagger]+II^\\dagger-J^\\dagger J.$$\n\nThe datum is stable if the vectors $B_{i_1}\\cdots B_{i_m}I(w)$, with $m\\ge0$, $i_j\\in\\{1,2\\}$ and $w\\in W$, span $V$. It is costable if there is no nonzero subspace of $V$ invariant under both $B_i$ and contained in $\\ker J$. It is regular if it is stable and costable. The group $GL(V)$ acts by $g\\cdot x=(gB_1g^{-1},gB_2g^{-1},gI,Jg^{-1})$. Its unitary subgroup is $U(V)$.\n\nWrite $X^s=\\{x:\\mu_{\\mathbb C}(x)=0,\\ x\\text{ stable}\\}$, $X^{\\mathrm{reg}}=\\{x\\in X^s:x\\text{ costable}\\}$ and, for real $\\zeta\\ge0$, $X_\\zeta^U=\\{x\\in X^s:\\mu_{\\mathbb R}(x)=\\zeta I_V\\}$. Denote the corresponding orbit sets by $X^s/GL(V)$, $X^{\\mathrm{reg}}/GL(V)$ and $X_\\zeta^U/U(V)$, with quotient topologies when discussing their analytic spaces. A word frame consists of $k$ columns obtained by fixed words in $B_1,B_2$ applied to fixed standard basis vectors of $W$.\n\nFor $T_{\\mathbb C}=(\\mathbb C^*)^2\\times(\\mathbb C^*)^N$, write $t_1,t_2,e_1,\\ldots,e_N$ for the coordinate characters and put $h=\\operatorname{diag}(e_1^{-1},\\ldots,e_N^{-1})$. Its action is $(t_1,t_2,h)\\cdot x=(t_1B_1,t_2B_2,Ih^{-1},t_1t_2hJ)$. The compact torus is its subgroup on which all characters have modulus one. The framing representation thus has character $\\sum_\\alpha e_\\alpha^{-1}$ and its dual has character $\\sum_\\alpha e_\\alpha$.\n\nThese are finite-dimensional matrix definitions. They do not assert a real/complex quotient correspondence, a sheaf or ASD identification, or a noncompact pushforward. In particular, positive-FI stability and zero-FI regularity are distinct conditions; neither an endpoint identification nor an ordinary ASD interpretation follows from this definition.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "2.2",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "A4",
          "source": "def-axiom-of-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Choice** (AC) is the following statement.\n\n> Every family of nonempty sets has a choice function\n> (Choice function).\n\nWritten out: for every set $\\mathcal{F}$ all of whose members are nonempty,\nthere exists a function $g$ with domain $\\mathcal{F}$ satisfying $g(S) \\in S$ for\nall $S \\in \\mathcal{F}$.\n\nAn equivalent formulation is that a product of nonempty sets is nonempty: if\n$X_i \\ne \\emptyset$ for every $i \\in I$, then $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i \\ne \\emptyset$.\nHere $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i$ is the set of functions $f$ with domain $I$ such that\n$f(i) \\in X_i$ for every $i \\in I$; when a family of nonempty sets is indexed by\nitself, such an $f$ is precisely a choice function for it.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "thm-qftx-framed-p2-monad-adhm-correspondence",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume AC. Let $V=\\mathbb C^k$, $W=\\mathbb C^N$, $N\\ge1$, $k\\ge0$, and let $(B_1,B_2,I,J)$ satisfy $[B_1,B_2]+IJ=0$ and be stable, meaning that the vectors obtained by applying all words in $B_1,B_2$ to $I(W)$ span $V$. Costable means that no nonzero common $B_1,B_2$-invariant subspace lies in $\\ker J$, and regular means stable and costable. On $\\mathbb P^2$ with coordinates $[z_0:z_1:z_2]$, define\n\n$$\\alpha=\\begin{pmatrix}z_0B_1-z_1\\\\z_0B_2-z_2\\\\z_0J\\end{pmatrix}:V\\otimes\\mathcal O(-1)\\to(V\\oplus V\\oplus W)\\otimes\\mathcal O,\\qquad \\beta=\\begin{pmatrix}-z_0B_2+z_2&z_0B_1-z_1&z_0I\\end{pmatrix}:(V\\oplus V\\oplus W)\\otimes\\mathcal O\\to V\\otimes\\mathcal O(1).$$\n\nThen $\\beta\\alpha=z_0^2([B_1,B_2]+IJ)=0$, $\\alpha$ is injective as a sheaf map, and $\\beta$ is surjective at every point. The three-term complex is a monad, meaning it is exact at its first and last terms; its middle cohomology $\\mathcal E=\\ker\\beta/\\operatorname{im}\\alpha$ is torsion-free of rank $N$, has Hilbert polynomial $N\\binom{m+2}{2}-k$, and has a canonical framing $\\mathcal E|_{L_\\infty}\\cong W\\otimes\\mathcal O_{L_\\infty}$. Every framed torsion-free sheaf with this rank, framing and Hilbert polynomial arises uniquely up to simultaneous change of basis in $V$ from this construction. For a finite-type complex scheme $S$, a family of ADHM data is a rank-$k$ vector bundle with endomorphisms and framing maps satisfying the equation and stable on every geometric fiber; its monad cohomology is flat over $S$, and the two constructions are inverse on those families for which the relative cohomology bundles and base-change maps in the preceding Beilinson lemma have the stated ranks and are isomorphisms. A first-order framed deformation means an isomorphism class of such a family over $\\mathbb C[\\epsilon]/(\\epsilon^2)$ with its special fiber identified; the correspondence induces inverse maps on those deformation spaces and their tangent vector spaces. The sheaf is locally free exactly when the ADHM datum is costable; hence the regular locus is the locally-free sheaf locus. Combined with the real/complex quotient theorem, positive-FI data correspond to all such framed torsion-free sheaves, while the zero-FI real quotient corresponds to the locally-free locus. The framing representation has character $\\sum_{a=1}^N e_a^{-1}$ and the dual framing has character $\\sum_{a=1}^N e_a$.",
          "uses": [
