          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Choice where the geometric suppliers of the named items use it. Let $G$ be a linearly reductive affine group variety over $k$ acting on a smooth variety $X$. Then the fixed-point subscheme $X^G$ is smooth (Milne, Ch. 13); if $S\\subseteq G(k)$ is Zariski-dense then $X^S=X^G$ and both are smooth (13.5-13.7); if $k$ is algebraically closed and $g\\in G(k)$ is semisimple, then the closure of the subgroup generated by $g$ is linearly reductive and $X^g=X^{G_0}$ is smooth (13.8). In particular, if a linearly reductive group $H$ acts on a smooth algebraic group $G$, then the fixed subgroup $G^H$ is smooth; when $H\\subseteq G$ acts by conjugation this is $C_G(H)$ (13.9), and for a subgroup $H\\subseteq G$ of multiplicative type the centralizer $C_G(H)$ and normalizer $N_G(H)$ are smooth, with no smoothness assumption on $H$. If $H$ is smooth, its geometric points are schematically dense, and these are also the unique smooth closed subgroup schemes whose geometric points are the centralizer and normalizer of $H(k^{\\mathrm a})$ (13.10-13.11). The pointwise identification is not asserted for nonsmooth $H$: for $H=\\mu_p$ embedded by $t\\mapsto\\operatorname{diag}(t,1)$ in $\\mathrm{GL}_2$ in characteristic $p$, $H(k^{\\mathrm a})=1$ but $C_G(H)$ is the diagonal torus.",
