      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "At a complex point, coherent algebraic and analytic modules are finite-dimensional complex vector spaces, so the absolute assertions hold in dimension zero. Flatness in [A1] makes analytification exact stalkwise, including for nonreduced finite-type schemes. Tensor associativity gives tensor compatibility and compatibility with quotient restriction to a hyperplane. A finite algebraic presentation analytifies to a finite analytic presentation, so [A3] ensures its cokernel is coherent. For a closed analytic embedding, direct image is exact on stalks and preserves flabby sheaves: its sections over an open are the original sections over the intersection with the closed subspace. Pushing forward a flabby resolution therefore identifies cohomology on the closed subspace and its direct image. These facts apply to the hyperplane sequences below.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "A3",
            "F5",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "Suppose the absolute theorem has been proved in dimensions below $r$, where $1\\le r\\le3$. For $\\mathcal O_{\\mathbb P^r}$ the natural comparison is an isomorphism by [A5] and [F3]. The exact hyperplane sequences $0\\to\\mathcal O(m-1)\\xrightarrow{x_0}\\mathcal O(m)\\to i_*\\mathcal O_H(m)\\to0$ exist on both sides by step 1.1, and their cohomology maps commute with analytification. For $m\\ge1$, assume comparison at $m-1$. The lower-dimensional comparison and [F3] give $H^q(H,\\mathcal O_H(m))=0$ for $q>0$; the sequence therefore gives analytic higher vanishing at $m$ and an exact sequence on global sections. Every homogeneous degree-$m$ polynomial on $H$ extends by the same polynomial in $x_1,\\ldots,x_r$ on $\\mathbb P^r$; the kernel of restriction is $x_0$ times the degree-$m-1$ polynomials. The natural map of these exact section sequences is an isomorphism at its outer terms, hence also at its middle term by [F4]. This proves the natural comparison for all $m\\ge0$ without using GAGA in dimension $r$.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "A5",
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "step 1.1",
            "induction"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "For the negative twists, the sequence $0\\to\\mathcal O(-1)\\to\\mathcal O\\to i_*\\mathcal O_H\\to0$ has restriction $\\mathbb C\\to\\mathbb C$ equal to the identity in degree zero and no positive cohomology in the two right terms by [A5] and lower-dimensional comparison. Thus analytic $\\mathcal O(-1)$ is acyclic, as is its algebraic counterpart by [F3]. For every $m<-1$, multiplication by a power of $x_0$ injects the line bundle $\\mathcal O(m)$ into $\\mathcal O(-1)$: in each analytic chart its local equation is a nonzero holomorphic function in the integral holomorphic germ ring, so multiplication is injective. Hence $H^0(\\mathcal O(m))=0$. Descend using $0\\to\\mathcal O(m)\\to\\mathcal O(m+1)\\to i_*\\mathcal O_H(m+1)\\to0$. Comparison is already known at $m+1$ and on the hyperplane at every twist. For $q\\ge1$, the four neighbors in $H^{q-1}(\\mathcal O(m+1))\\to H^{q-1}(\\mathcal O_H(m+1))\\to H^q(\\mathcal O(m))\\to H^q(\\mathcal O(m+1))\\to H^q(\\mathcal O_H(m+1))$ are therefore naturally compared isomorphically, so [F4] gives the middle comparison. This also handles $r=1$, where the point hyperplane's negative twists still have a one-dimensional section space; they were not incorrectly set to zero. Thus all integer twists have canonical cohomology comparison, without analytic Serre duality or an affine finite-pole assertion.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "A5",
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "step 1.1",
            "step 2.1",
            "induction"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-4-1",
          "claim": "Resolve an algebraic coherent $\\mathcal F$ by [F2]. Its finite twisting-sheaf resolution stays exact analytically by step 1.1. Starting at its leftmost term, the long exact sequences and steps 2.1 and 3.1 prove cohomology comparison for each successive cokernel and hence for $\\mathcal F$ in every degree. This uses the actual finite resolution, not an unproved global analytic free resolution.",
          "step": "4.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F4",
            "step 1.1",
            "step 2.1",
            "step 3.1",
            "induction"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-4-2",
          "claim": "Let $\\mathcal M$ now be any coherent analytic sheaf on $\\mathbb P^r$. For every nonzero linear section $s$, retain both $K_s=\\ker(\\mathcal M(-1)\\xrightarrow{s}\\mathcal M)$ and $Q_s=\\operatorname{coker}(s)$, and write $I_s=\\operatorname{im}(s)$. Locally $s$ is a hyperplane equation, so it annihilates $K_s$ and $Q_s$; [A3] makes them coherent sheaves on $H_s\\cong\\mathbb P^{r-1}$. The inductive equivalence algebraizes them. By [F3], their positive cohomology vanishes after sufficiently positive twists. The two exact sequences $0\\to K_s(n)\\to\\mathcal M(n-1)\\to I_s(n)\\to0$ and $0\\to I_s(n)\\to\\mathcal M(n)\\to Q_s(n)\\to0$ then show that multiplication by $s$ on $H^q(\\mathcal M(n-1))$ is eventually an isomorphism for $q\\ge2$, and eventually surjective for $q=1$. The $H^0(Q_s(n))$ term prevents an initial claim of injectivity in degree one. This argument includes hyperplane torsion; $s$ was not assumed injective on $\\mathcal M$.",
