          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-5-2",
          "claim": "Fix $q>0$ and a nonzero $s_0$. For $q=1$, the eventual surjections in step 4.2 make the finite dimensions in [A4] nonincreasing integers, hence eventually constant; thereafter multiplication by $s_0$ is an isomorphism. For $q\\ge2$ it is already eventually an isomorphism. Choose $N$ beyond this range, put $V=H^q(\\mathcal M(N))$, and identify each $H^q(\\mathcal M(n))$, $n\\ge N$, with $V$ by successive multiplication by $s_0$. A second independent section $s_1$ induces an endomorphism $T_n$ on $V$ between successive degrees. The commuting multiplication square gives $T_{n+1}=T_n=T$. If $V\\ne0$, choose an eigenvalue $\\lambda$ by [F7]. Multiplication by the nonzero section $s_1-\\lambda s_0$ then induces $T-\\lambda I$, singular at every such degree. But step 4.2 applied to this fixed section is eventually an isomorphism for $q\\ge2$; for $q=1$ it is eventually surjective between spaces of the same stabilized dimension and hence an isomorphism too. This contradiction proves eventual vanishing. The eigenvalue is chosen before its section's threshold; no bound uniform over an infinite pencil is used.",
          "step": "5.2",
          "inputs": [
