          "claim": "The Beilinson construction is natural under framed sheaf isomorphisms, so an isomorphism of framed sheaves induces maps on the cohomology vector spaces used in steps 1.2–6.2 and hence a single change of basis $g\\in GL(V)$; the normalized Euler blocks force the recovered tuples to differ by $g\\cdot(B_1,B_2,I,J)$. Conversely such a $g$ is an isomorphism of monads acting trivially on $W$, and therefore induces a framed sheaf isomorphism. This proves the pointwise inverse equivalence modulo $GL(V)$. For a finite-type data family, step 2.1 makes $\\beta$ surjective on the total space, so $K=\\ker\\beta$ is locally free. The sequence $0\\to V\\mathcal O(-1)\\to K\\to\\mathcal E\\to0$ is exact and its terms before $\\mathcal E$ are flat over the base. After tensoring with any residue field of the base, the first map remains sheaf-injective by step 1.1; hence $\\operatorname{Tor}_1(k(s),\\mathcal E)=0$. Since $\\mathcal E$ is finite over each local ring of $\\mathbb P^2\\times S$ and its residue fiber is a vector space over the residue field, [F8] gives flatness over $S$. Under the stated relative cohomology and base-change hypotheses, the same constructions use vector bundles and are inverse for families.",
