          "id": "proof-1-1",
          "step": "1.1",
          "claim": "Since stability says $I\\ne0$, $IJ=0$ forces $J=0$. The real equation is then $|I|^2=\\zeta$, so $I$ lies on the circle of radius $\\sqrt\\zeta$. The $U(1)$ gauge action multiplies $I$ by its phase and leaves scalar $B_1,B_2$ unchanged. It is transitive on that circle with trivial stabilizer; hence the quotient is exactly the unconstrained pair $(B_1,B_2)\\in\\mathbb C^2$. By [F2], this is the positive-FI stable quotient.",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "proof-2-1",
          "step": "2.1",
          "claim": "At zero real level, $J=0$ still follows from $I\\ne0$ and $IJ=0$, while the real equation becomes $|I|^2=0$. This contradicts $I\\ne0$, so the stable zero-level locus is empty. In the positive chamber, $J=0$ makes all of $V$ a nonzero invariant subspace in $\\ker J$; the data are not costable. By [F3], their framed sheaves are not locally free.",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "step 1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "proof-3-1",
          "step": "3.1",
          "claim": "Let $P\\to S^4$ be any smooth principal $U(1)$ bundle. Smooth local connection forms glue by a smooth partition of unity [F5]. Parallel transport from the center of each closed hemisphere along radial segments gives a smooth trivialization: each transported frame solves a linear smooth ODE, and [F5] supplies its smooth dependence on the endpoint. Their transition is a map $g:S^3\\to U(1)$. Pulling back the universal cover $\\mathbb R\\to U(1)$ gives a covering of $S^3$; its connected component through a chosen point is a connected cover, hence is one-sheeted by [F4]. Thus $g$ lifts to $\\theta:S^3\\to\\mathbb R$. Since the covering is a local diffeomorphism and $g$ is smooth, the lift is smooth. The homotopy $\\theta_s=(1-s)\\theta+s\\theta(x_0)$ makes $g$ null-homotopic. Reparameterize this homotopy to be constant near its final parameter and use it on a collar of one hemisphere; this changes that trivialization so the transition is constant and the local frames glue to a global frame. Hence $P$ is trivial. In that frame write the real connection form as $a$ and its curvature as $f=da$, so $df=0$. Then $f\\wedge f=d(a\\wedge f)$, and Stokes [F6] gives $\\int_{S^4}f\\wedge f=0$. If it is ASD, $f=-*f$, so $f\\wedge f=-f\\wedge *f=-|f|^2d\\mathrm{vol}$. The zero integral forces $f=0$, and the charge in the Statement is zero. Thus no smooth charge-one $U(1)$ ASD connection exists, and that empty ordinary sector cannot equal the positive-FI quotient $\\mathbb C^2$.",
          "inputs": [
            "F4",
            "F5",
