          "source": "thm-qftx-projective-gaga-for-monad-data",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For $n=2$ or $3$, let $X=\\mathbb P^n_{\\mathbb C}$ and let $X^{an}$ be its complex analytic space and $\\eta:X^{an}\\to X$ the canonical morphism of ringed spaces. Define analytification on $X^{an}$ by $\\mathcal F^{an}=\\eta^{-1}\\mathcal F\\otimes_{\\eta^{-1}\\mathcal O_X}\\mathcal O_{X^{an}}$. It is an exact tensor-compatible equivalence from algebraic coherent sheaves (locally finitely presented $\\mathcal O_X$-modules) to holomorphic coherent sheaves. It identifies algebraic and holomorphic morphisms, preserves cohomology and the derived morphism groups $\\operatorname{Ext}^q$, and commutes with restriction to the fixed hyperplane $L_\\infty=\\{z_0=0\\}$. For an algebraic coherent sheaf $\\mathcal E$ flat over a finite-type complex parameter scheme $S$, let $p:X\\times S\\to S$ be projection. Analytification commutes with the algebraic family structure and fiber restrictions. Whenever the algebraic and analytic higher direct images $R^qp_*\\mathcal E$ are locally free and the natural maps from their residue-field fibers to $H^q(X_s,\\mathcal E_s)$ are isomorphisms, the comparison identifies those direct images and base-change maps. No assertion is made that an arbitrary holomorphic family over a nonproper algebraic base algebraizes.",
