        {
          "id": "step-5-1",
          "claim": "To identify the connection with the first-neighborhood prescription, the line's conormal bundle is O(-1)², by its two independent linear defining equations. The sequence 0→E|L⊗(I/I²)→E|L^(1)→E|L→0 and [F3]'s O(-1) vanishings give a unique extension of every line section to L^(1). Smooth variation is supplied by step 3.1, not merely inferred from this pointwise uniqueness. At a graph X choose a local holomorphic section s of H with specified value and ∇s(X)=0; in a local frame its affine-linear coefficient function can realize any prescribed first jet. Its evaluation derivative in parameter direction v vanishes on vλ=0 by step 3.1, so factors through the normal displacement vλ. These factorizations glue on the two λ charts and give precisely a first-neighborhood extension. Uniqueness identifies it with the preceding extension. Subtracting such a horizontal first jet from an arbitrary section shows that differentiation relative to this extension is exactly ∇. This proves the prescription, its Leibniz rule, chart independence and smooth dependence.",
