          "id": "step-2-1",
          "step": "2.1",
          "claim": "Substituting that representative in [F3] gives $\\alpha=(-z_1,-z_2,0)^T$ and $\\beta=(z_2,-z_1,z_0)$. Their product is $-z_2z_1+z_1z_2=0$. The row $\\beta$ is surjective at every projective point because its three entries have no common zero, and $\\alpha$ is injective as a sheaf map because it is nonzero away from $p$. Let $K=\\ker\\beta$. Projection to the third coordinate restricts to a morphism $\\gamma:K\\to\\mathcal O$. On the affine chart $z_0=1$, put $x=z_1$ and $y=z_2$; a vector in $K$ has the form $(u,v,-yu+xv)$, so $\\gamma(K)=(x,y)$ and its kernel is generated by $(-x,-y,0)$, the image of $\\alpha$. On any chart away from $p$ where $z_1$ or $z_2$ is a unit, the same equations show that $\\gamma$ is onto and its kernel is generated by $\\alpha$. Hence $K/\\operatorname{im}\\alpha\\cong I_p$ globally, where $I_p$ is the ideal sheaf of $p$.",
