          "step": "2.2",
          "claim": "Expanding $[A,C]+(I\\lambda_1-J^\\dagger\\lambda_2)(J\\lambda_1+I^\\dagger\\lambda_2)$ gives the stated polynomial with coefficients $\\mu_{\\mathbb C},\\mu_{\\mathbb R},-\\mu_{\\mathbb C}^\\dagger$. Substitution of $\\sigma v$ in $\\alpha$, followed by adjoint, gives exactly $(-C,A,I\\lambda_1-J^\\dagger\\lambda_2)=\\beta(v)$. On a real line the same substitution gives $\\alpha=a_z\\lambda_1+b_z\\lambda_2$ and $\\beta=b_z^\\dagger\\lambda_1-a_z^\\dagger\\lambda_2$. The scalar-coordinate matrix $\\left(\\begin{smallmatrix}\\lambda_1&-\\bar\\lambda_2\\\\\\lambda_2&\\bar\\lambda_1\\end{smallmatrix}\\right)$ is invertible whenever $(\\lambda_1,\\lambda_2)\\ne0$. Solving the two displayed incidence equations similarly recovers a unique $z$ from each point with $\\lambda\\ne0$; their real-linear coefficient matrix has determinant $(|\\lambda_1|^2+|\\lambda_2|^2)^2$. The points with $\\lambda=0$ form $L_\\infty$. Thus these lines cover $\\mathbb P^3$.",
          "inputs": [
