        {
          "id": "step-4-2",
          "step": "4.2",
          "claim": "For regular zero-moment data the maps $a_z,b_z$ have orthogonal images and equal positive Gram matrix $f_z$. Therefore $a_z\\lambda_1+b_z\\lambda_2$ is injective for every nonzero $\\lambda$, since its squared norm on $v$ is $(|\\lambda_1|^2+|\\lambda_2|^2)\\langle v,f_zv\\rangle$. The identity with $\\sigma$ makes $\\beta$ surjective as well. At $L_\\infty$, $\\alpha=(-\\mu_1,-\\mu_2,0)^T$ and $\\beta=(\\mu_2,-\\mu_1,0)$ have the same properties. A nonzero maximal minor locally solves each kernel and image equation by holomorphic matrix inversion, so their kernels, images and middle quotient have holomorphic local frames. Hence the middle cohomology is a holomorphic bundle. On a finite real line, split $K=\\operatorname{im}a_z\\oplus\\operatorname{im}b_z\\oplus\\ker D_z$. Using $a_z,b_z$ to identify the first two summands with $V\\oplus V$, and multiplying the target by $f_z^{-1}$, the first two summands form $V$ copies of the Euler complex $\\mathcal O(-1)\\xrightarrow{(\\lambda_1,\\lambda_2)}\\mathcal O^2\\xrightarrow{(-\\lambda_2,\\lambda_1)}\\mathcal O(1)$, whose middle cohomology is zero. The remaining summand is the constant $\\ker D_z$, proving the canonical linewise identification. The identical Euler calculation at infinity leaves $W$ with its standard framing.",
